# Copy to .env and fill in. Run: set -a; . ./.env; set +a; ./target/release/botd

# Required for remote access and recommended on loopback. Browser login: any username, this password.
# botd refuses to start with an empty password unless HOST is loopback and ALLOW_NO_AUTH=1.
AUTH_PASSWORD=
# Or AUTH_PASSWORD_FILE=/path/to/file (Docker and systemd secrets). Not both.
# Bots never see this password: it's removed from their environment, so they can't approve their own actions.
# To run operator commands (botctl policy, approve, deny, audit), export SUPERBOT_PASSWORD=<this> in your
# own shell. Don't add SUPERBOT_PASSWORD to this file.

# Master key that encrypts the database (see SUPERBOT_DATABASE_ENCRYPTION) and the secrets botd
# saves in it (botctl secret set, /api/secrets).
# Default: ~/.config/superbot/master.key, created on first start (0600). The installer sets the
# file path below. Or put the key itself in SUPERBOT_MASTER_KEY (64 hex chars or base64 of 32 bytes,
# e.g. `openssl rand -hex 32`). Not both. Keep it out of DATA_DIR and back it up apart from the
# database: without it, an encrypted database can't be opened at all. docs/adr/0010-secrets.md
# SUPERBOT_MASTER_KEY_FILE=
# SUPERBOT_MASTER_KEY=
# Lost the key for good, with a plain database? Start once with SUPERBOT_FORGET_SEALED=1: saved
# secrets are dropped, the rest of the database is kept, and a new key is made. Remove it
# afterwards. An encrypted database can't be recovered without its key: restore one from a backup.
# Database encryption at rest (docs/adr/0116-database-encrypted-at-rest.md): SQLCipher, with a key
# derived from the master key. auto (default): a new database is encrypted, an existing plain one
# stays plain, with a warning in the log, the setup checklist and /metrics. required: a plain
# database is encrypted when botd starts, keeping a plain copy beside it (app.sqlite-plain-<time>)
# until you delete it; botd refuses to start if it can't. off: a new database is plain, by choice.
# An encrypted database is always opened with its key, whatever this says. The installer and
# deploy/docker/init.sh write required for new installs. docs/ops/database-encryption.md
# SUPERBOT_DATABASE_ENCRYPTION=auto

# External secrets backend (docs/adr/0125-external-secrets-backend-vault.md, docs/ops/vault.md).
# local (default): the environment and the sealed store only. vault: also read secrets from
# HashiCorp Vault (or OpenBao) KV v2, one secret per name with the value in field "value":
# <mount>/<prefix>/bots/<NAME> for keys Bots get, <mount>/<prefix>/server/<NAME> for botd's own
# (LINODE_TOKEN, SSO_*_CLIENT_SECRET, SMTP_PASSWORD, STRIPE_SECRET_KEY, ...). Order: this file wins,
# then Vault, then saved secrets. Answers are cached for SECRETS_CACHE_SECONDS (0-86400, default 300).
# Fails closed: botd won't start if it can't reach Vault, and once the cache runs out a Vault that
# can't answer stops Bot runs rather than using an old or saved value. Every read that reaches Vault
# is audited (secret.read). VAULT_ADDR is https (http only to loopback, e.g. Vault Agent).
# VAULT_AUTH=token uses VAULT_TOKEN or VAULT_TOKEN_FILE (read again when Vault refuses the token);
# VAULT_AUTH=approle uses VAULT_ROLE_ID(_FILE) and VAULT_SECRET_ID(_FILE). Bots never get these.
# SECRETS_BACKEND=local
# SECRETS_CACHE_SECONDS=300
# VAULT_ADDR=https://vault.example.com:8200
# VAULT_NAMESPACE=
# VAULT_CACERT=
# VAULT_AUTH=token
# VAULT_TOKEN_FILE=
# VAULT_APPROLE_MOUNT=approle
# VAULT_ROLE_ID_FILE=
# VAULT_SECRET_ID_FILE=
# VAULT_KV_MOUNT=secret
# VAULT_KV_PREFIX=superbot
# VAULT_KV_FIELD=value

# Azure Key Vault (SECRETS_BACKEND=azure-keyvault) and GCP Secret Manager (gcp-secret-manager)
# work the same way (docs/adr/0136-cloud-secret-managers.md, docs/ops/cloud-secrets.md): one secret
# per name, <prefix>-bots-<NAME> and <prefix>-server-<NAME> (on Azure, _ is written -). Azure signs in
# with a managed identity (default), a service principal (client-secret) or AKS workload identity;
# GCP with the metadata server (default) or a service account key. Bots never get these credentials.
# AZURE_KEYVAULT_URL=https://my-vault.vault.azure.net
# AZURE_KEYVAULT_PREFIX=superbot
# AZURE_KEYVAULT_AUTH=managed-identity
# AZURE_CLIENT_ID=
# AZURE_TENANT_ID=
# AZURE_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE=
# GCP_SECRET_MANAGER_PROJECT=
# GCP_SECRET_MANAGER_LOCATION=
# GCP_SECRET_MANAGER_PREFIX=superbot
# GCP_SECRET_MANAGER_AUTH=metadata
# GCP_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_KEY_FILE=

# AWS Secrets Manager (SECRETS_BACKEND=aws) works the same way (docs/adr/0146-aws-secrets-manager.md,
# docs/ops/cloud-secrets.md#aws-secrets-manager): <prefix>/bots/<NAME> and <prefix>/server/<NAME>, as plain
# text. Credentials come from AWS's chain, picked once at startup: AWS_ACCESS_KEY_ID and AWS_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY,
# web identity (EKS), container credentials (ECS, EKS Pod Identity) or the instance profile (IMDSv2). Pin one
# with AWS_SECRETS_MANAGER_AUTH=env|web-identity|container|instance-profile. Bots never get botd's credentials.
# AWS_SECRETS_MANAGER_REGION=eu-west-2
# AWS_SECRETS_MANAGER_PREFIX=superbot
# AWS_SECRETS_MANAGER_AUTH=chain

# API keys for Bots (ANTHROPIC_API_KEY, OPENAI_API_KEY, GITHUB_TOKEN, ...) can go here as plain
# variables (or NAME_FILE=/path), or be saved encrypted with `botctl secret set NAME < file`.
# Variables here win over saved ones with the same name.

# Dev only (loopback): allow unauthenticated API access — logs a loud warning. Never on a public HOST.
ALLOW_NO_AUTH=
HOST=127.0.0.1
PORT=3000
# Extra allowed Host headers, comma-separated (case-insensitive). HOST:PORT and localhost variants are automatic.
# Behind HTTPS, add your public name, e.g. bots.example.com
ALLOWED_HOSTS=

# CORS for apps/client (web/Electron). Default: Expo dev origins. Use * only on trusted LANs.
# CORS_ORIGINS=http://localhost:8081,https://your-app.example
CORS_ORIGINS=

# Your name, shown in the app and told to the Bots.
USER_NAME=

# claude, codex, pi, or hermes. New Bots start on this harness.
DEFAULT_HARNESS=claude
# Hermes Agent only (docs/backend.md): its --provider and --base-url. Empty = Hermes's own config.
# The base URL must be https://, or http:// only to this host (127.0.0.1, ::1, localhost) (ADR 0097).
SUPERBOT_HERMES_PROVIDER=
SUPERBOT_HERMES_BASE_URL=

# Where the database, Bot memory, and harness sessions live. Back this up.
DATA_DIR=./data
# The Bots' shared working folder (their "computer").
WORKSPACE_DIR=./workspace

# Server time zone. Routines run on it.
TZ=UTC

# Max minutes for one task before it is stopped.
RUN_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=60

# Bot runs at once on this server (docs/adr/0073-concurrent-runs-per-machine.md); more wait their turn,
# oldest first, and the Bot shows it's queued. Also the cap on a user's machine whose tier sets no
# concurrentRuns. Empty: 8. 0 or off: no cap.
MAX_CONCURRENT_RUNS=

# Audit log (docs/adr/0009-audit-events.md): days to keep events, 1 to 36500. Empty or 0 = keep forever (default).
# Older events are deleted every 6 hours; backups still hold them for SUPERBOT_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS.
AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS=

# Audit SIEM export (docs/ops/audit-export.md, ADR 0122), off by default: every audit event, in order, as JSON
# lines POSTed over https, or as RFC 5424 syslog over TLS (syslog+tls://host[:6514]). Plain http:// and
# syslog+tcp:// only to loopback, or with AUDIT_EXPORT_INSECURE=on. AUDIT_EXPORT_HEADERS (or _FILE) is a
# secret Bots never get. AUDIT_EXPORT_START=new skips the history already in the database (default all).
# Undelivered events are retried with backoff and kept past AUDIT_RETENTION_DAYS until delivered.
# AUDIT_EXPORT=https://<siem-host>/<path>
# AUDIT_EXPORT_HEADERS=Authorization=Bearer <token>
# AUDIT_EXPORT_INSECURE=off
# AUDIT_EXPORT_START=all

# Chat history (docs/adr/0098-chat-retention.md): days to keep messages, 1 to 36500. Empty or 0 = keep
# forever (default). Older messages are deleted every 6 hours, in batches, except a run still going and
# Bots an admin holds (botctl retention hold); backups still hold them for SUPERBOT_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS.
CHAT_RETENTION_DAYS=

# Harness session files (docs/adr/0112-harness-session-and-teammate-files-retention.md): days to keep
# Claude Code, Codex, Pi and Hermes Agent sessions nothing has changed for, 1 to 36500. Empty or 0 = keep
# forever (default). Swept every 6 hours on this server, in per-user accounts and on users' machines; never a
# running Bot's, nor anything in a HOME where a held Bot runs.
HARNESS_SESSION_RETENTION_DAYS=

# Teammates' archived work (docs/adr/0112-harness-session-and-teammate-files-retention.md): days to keep a
# leaving teammate's work in the owner's from-teammates/ folder, 1 to 36500. Empty or 0 = keep forever
# (default). Never while one of the owner's Bots is held.
TEAMMATE_FILES_RETENTION_DAYS=

# SOC 2 evidence bundles (docs/adr/0142-type-ii-evidence-bundles.md, docs/security/soc2-evidence-plan.md).
# monthly: on the 1st, botd writes last month's bundle (access review, versions, backups, retention,
# sign-ins and alerts, with SHA-256 digests; no secrets, names, emails or chats) into EVIDENCE_DIR
# (default DATA_DIR/evidence), audited as evidence.bundle_created. Off (default): make them with
# `botctl evidence bundle`. Bundles are deleted EVIDENCE_RETENTION_DAYS after their month ends
# (default 760: a 12-month observation period plus a year; 0 = keep forever). Copy them to the
# evidence store; they are also in the DATA_DIR backups.
# EVIDENCE_BUNDLES=monthly
# EVIDENCE_DIR=
# EVIDENCE_RETENTION_DAYS=760

# Metrics and logs (deploy/README.md#metrics-and-logs, docs/adr/0089-metrics-and-structured-logs.md).
# Prometheus /metrics on a listener of its own, never the main port: off (default), on (127.0.0.1:9464),
# or an address and port. Off loopback it needs METRICS_TOKEN (or METRICS_TOKEN_FILE), 24+ characters.
# METRICS_LISTEN=on
# METRICS_TOKEN_FILE=/home/superbot/superbot/metrics-token
# text (default) or json: one JSON object per line, with request and run ids.
# LOG_FORMAT=json
# Error reporting (deploy/README.md#metrics-and-logs, docs/adr/0113-service-dashboards-and-error-reporting.md):
# off by default. A Sentry-compatible DSN (Sentry, GlitchTip, Bugsink; self-hosted works) gets each panic and
# 5xx: route template, status, source location, request id and version only, never a prompt, chat, path or body.
# https only (plain http only to loopback). Or ERROR_REPORT_DSN_FILE. Environment tag: production by default.
# ERROR_REPORT_DSN=https://<key>@<error-tracker-host>/<project-id>
# ERROR_REPORT_ENVIRONMENT=production
# Traces (deploy/README.md#traces, docs/ops/tracing.md, docs/adr/0118-harness-run-traces-and-per-tier-metrics.md):
# off by default. An OTLP/HTTP collector gets a span per request and Bot run, with harness attempts, tool calls,
# approval checks and machine calls as children: ids, route templates, results and token counts only, never a
# prompt, chat, tool input, path or secret. https only (plain http only to loopback, or with TRACES_OTLP_INSECURE=on).
# TRACES_OTLP_HEADERS (or TRACES_OTLP_HEADERS_FILE) is a secret Bots never get. Ratio 0 to 1 (default 1).
# TRACES_PARENT=accept continues a proxy's traceparent header (ignored by default).
# TRACES_OTLP_ENDPOINT=https://<collector-host>:4318
# TRACES_OTLP_HEADERS=Authorization=Bearer <token>
# TRACES_SAMPLE_RATIO=0.25
# TRACES_PARENT=accept
# The public status page at /status and /status.json (deploy/README.md#status-page-and-slo-alerts,
# docs/adr/0090-status-page-and-uptime-slo.md): on (default) or off.
# STATUS_PAGE=off

# Backups (the installer's daily superbot-backup timer; docs/ops/backup-restore.md). Archives go to
# ~/superbot/backups unless BACKUP_DIR is set; kept SUPERBOT_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS (default 14).
# Encrypt them with age: public keys (age1..., comma-separated) or a recipients file. Keep the
# matching identity off this server; restoring needs it. A new install makes a key and sets
# this (the private half at /root/superbot-backup-identity.txt, to copy off the host).
# BACKUP_DIR=
# SUPERBOT_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14
# SUPERBOT_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS=age1...
# SUPERBOT_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS_FILE=
# Plain backups (docs/adr/0114-backups-encrypted-by-default.md): auto (default) warns at startup,
# in the setup checklist and in /metrics; required refuses to write or start with any backup
# plain (server archives, their off-host copies, machines' backups); off keeps them plain by
# choice, still shown in /metrics and the audit log.
# SUPERBOT_BACKUP_ENCRYPTION=auto
# Copies of those archives off this host (docs/adr/0109-server-backups-off-the-host.md): botd
# copies the newest to S3-compatible storage within a minute of superbot-backup writing it,
# with a manifest, and alerts when the newest copy is older than SERVER_BACKUP_RPO_HOURS.
# Without a store archives stay on this host, and botd warns about it (log, setup checklist,
# botctl backups, superbot_server_backup_off_host 0). Unset, the machine backups' store
# (MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_*, below) is used under its prefix's server/. Archives not encrypted
# above are encrypted on the way to SERVER_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS when set.
# auto copies when a store is set; on refuses to start without one; off never copies.
# SERVER_BACKUP_OFF_HOST=auto
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=<bucket>
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<s3-endpoint>
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<access-key-id>
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<secret-access-key>
# SERVER_BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=superbot/
# SERVER_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS=age1...
# The RPO defaults to the archive interval plus 2 hours (26). Retention: days, or off to leave
# deleting copies to the bucket's own lifecycle rules (then the keys need no delete right).
# SERVER_BACKUP_RPO_HOURS=26
# SERVER_BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=30
# 1 = take the client IP for the audit log from X-Forwarded-For whatever the peer. Only when botd is reachable
# solely through your proxy. A proxy on loopback (the installer's Caddy) is trusted without this.
TRUST_FORWARDED_FOR=

# Users and sessions (docs/adr/0012-users-sessions-rbac.md). AUTH_PASSWORD is the owner's break-glass
# login while AUTH_BREAK_GLASS is on (default). Set 0 once admins sign in another way; 1 to get back in.
AUTH_BREAK_GLASS=
# Two-factor authentication for sign-in codes and the password (docs/adr/0094-totp-second-factor.md):
# off, admins (the owner included), or all. Unset lets admins choose in the console; set, it can't be changed there.
AUTH_MFA=
# Email of the owner account, made on first start with USER_NAME.
OWNER_EMAIL=
# Session lifetimes: since last use (default 7d) and since sign-in (default 30d). E.g. 30m, 12h, 7d.
SESSION_IDLE_TIMEOUT=
SESSION_MAX_AGE=
# Suspicious sign-in alerts (docs/adr/0104-suspicious-sign-in-alerts.md): on (default) or off. Reported as
# auth.suspicious_sign_in audit events and metrics; bursts at one account and sign-ins right after a burst also
# go to the admins' email and webhook alerts. Failed guesses from one IP (default 10) or at one account from any
# IP (default 5) within the window (default 15m, up to 24h). New-network sign-ins reported for: admins (default), all, off.
# SIGNIN_ALERTS=on
# SIGNIN_ALERT_IP_FAILURES=10
# SIGNIN_ALERT_ACCOUNT_FAILURES=5
# SIGNIN_ALERT_WINDOW=15m
# SIGNIN_ALERT_NEW_IP=admins

# Single sign-on (docs/sso/, docs/adr/0014-oidc-sso-entra.md, docs/adr/0016-sso-multi-provider.md,
# docs/adr/0018-sso-github-apple.md, docs/adr/0020-saml-bridge.md).
# Off while SSO_PROVIDERS is empty. Several at once, one button each: SSO_PROVIDERS=entra,google,github,apple,adfs
# SSO_PUBLIC_URL is the https address people open SuperBot at; register
# {SSO_PUBLIC_URL}/api/auth/sso/<id>/callback as the redirect URI at each IdP.
SSO_PROVIDERS=
SSO_PUBLIC_URL=
# SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_ID=<application-client-id>
# SSO_ENTRA_TENANT=<directory-tenant-id>
# The client secret: SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET, SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET_FILE, or (recommended)
# `botctl secret set SSO_ENTRA_CLIENT_SECRET < secret.txt`. Bots never get it.
# Who an unknown Microsoft account becomes on first sign-in: off (default: an admin links people
# with `botctl user link`), readonly, member, or admin.
# SSO_ENTRA_JIT_ROLE=off

# Google (docs/sso/google.md): consumer and Workspace accounts. Secret: SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET (as above).
# SSO_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>.apps.googleusercontent.com
# Only these Workspace domains, checked on the token's hd claim. Needed for SSO_GOOGLE_JIT_ROLE.
# SSO_GOOGLE_HOSTED_DOMAINS=example.com
# SSO_GOOGLE_JIT_ROLE=off

# Any OpenID Connect IdP (docs/sso/oidc.md), here with the id okta. Secret: SSO_OKTA_CLIENT_SECRET.
# SSO_OKTA_TYPE=oidc
# SSO_OKTA_ISSUER=https://<org>.okta.com/oauth2/default
# SSO_OKTA_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
# SSO_OKTA_LABEL=Okta
# Only verified emails in these domains (needed for SSO_OKTA_JIT_ROLE); claim names if the IdP's
# differ (subject must never change).
# SSO_OKTA_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# SSO_OKTA_SUBJECT_CLAIM=sub
# SSO_OKTA_EMAIL_CLAIM=email
# SSO_OKTA_NAME_CLAIM=name
# SSO_OKTA_JIT_ROLE=off

# GitHub (docs/sso/github.md): an OAuth app, signed in by numeric user id with the verified
# primary email. Secret: SSO_GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET (as above).
# SSO_GITHUB_CLIENT_ID=<client-id>
# Only active members of these orgs, checked on GitHub's API. Needed for SSO_GITHUB_JIT_ROLE.
# SSO_GITHUB_ALLOWED_ORGS=<org>
# GitHub Enterprise Server: its web address (the API defaults to {URL}/api/v3).
# SSO_GITHUB_URL=https://github.com
# SSO_GITHUB_JIT_ROLE=off

# Apple (docs/sso/apple.md): a Services ID, and a Sign in with Apple key (.p8) that signs a
# short-lived client secret for each sign-in. The key: SSO_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY(_FILE), or
# (recommended) `botctl secret set SSO_APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY < AuthKey_<key-id>.p8`.
# SSO_APPLE_CLIENT_ID=<services-id>
# SSO_APPLE_TEAM_ID=<team-id>
# SSO_APPLE_KEY_ID=<key-id>
# Only verified emails in these domains (Hide My Email addresses never match). Needed for JIT.
# SSO_APPLE_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# SSO_APPLE_JIT_ROLE=off

# Native SAML 2.0 (Okta, Entra ID, ADFS, OneLogin; docs/sso/saml-native.md, docs/adr/0127-native-saml-service-provider.md):
# botd is the service provider and checks the IdP's signed responses itself. No secret unless you bring
# your own SP key (below). Here with the id corp.
# Leave out ISSUER (that is the bridge below). The IdP's metadata comes from a URL, a file, or
# `botctl sso saml corp import < metadata.xml`; botctl sso saml corp shows what to enter at the IdP.
# SSO_CORP_TYPE=saml
# SSO_CORP_IDP_ENTITY_ID=http://www.okta.com/<app-id>
# SSO_CORP_IDP_METADATA_URL=https://<idp-host>/<metadata-path>
# SSO_CORP_LABEL=Company SSO
# Only these email domains (from the email attribute). Needed for SSO_CORP_JIT_ROLE.
# SSO_CORP_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# SSO_CORP_JIT_ROLE=off
# Sign AuthnRequests, for IdPs that want them, with the SP key botd makes once and keeps sealed (ADR 0137).
# Single logout needs no setting: it is on when the IdP's metadata has a logout URL.
# SSO_CORP_SIGN_REQUESTS=false
# Or your own SP certificate, with its key as the secret SSO_CORP_PRIVATE_KEY.
# SSO_CORP_SP_CERT_FILE=/etc/superbot/corp-sp.pem

# SAML-only IdPs such as ADFS (docs/sso/saml.md, docs/adr/0020-saml-bridge.md), through a Keycloak
# realm that speaks SAML to the IdP and OpenID Connect to SuperBot. Here with the id adfs.
# Secret: SSO_ADFS_CLIENT_SECRET, the Keycloak client's (as above).
# SSO_ADFS_TYPE=saml
# SSO_ADFS_ISSUER=https://<keycloak-host>/realms/<realm>
# SSO_ADFS_CLIENT_ID=<keycloak-client-id>
# Only people this IdP (its alias in Keycloak) signed in, stored under its SAML entity ID.
# SSO_ADFS_IDP_ALIAS=adfs
# SSO_ADFS_IDP_ENTITY_ID=http://<adfs-host>/adfs/services/trust
# SSO_ADFS_LABEL=Company SSO
# Only verified emails in these domains. Needed for SSO_ADFS_JIT_ROLE.
# SSO_ADFS_ALLOWED_DOMAINS=example.com
# SSO_ADFS_JIT_ROLE=off

# Sign-ups from the get-started page (docs/adr/0022-landing-and-signups.md). An admin approves
# each one; nothing is emailed. off closes the form.
SIGNUPS=on
# Optional Cloudflare Turnstile bot check: set both, or neither. The secret can be a _FILE.
# TURNSTILE_SITE_KEY=<site-key>
# TURNSTILE_SECRET=<secret-key>

# SCIM provisioning (docs/sso/scim.md, docs/adr/0023-scim.md): Entra ID or Okta create, update,
# and deactivate users at https://<your-host>/scim/v2 with a token from `botctl scim token create`.
# Off by default (/scim/v2 answers 404). Users SCIM makes get SCIM_DEFAULT_ROLE: member or readonly.
# Deactivating or deleting someone stops their Bots' runs at once and suspends their machine
# (MACHINE_ON_DISABLE below, docs/adr/0124-deprovisioning-stops-bots-and-machines.md).
SCIM=off
# SCIM_DEFAULT_ROLE=member
# SCIM groups as teams (docs/adr/0095-scim-groups-as-teams.md): with SCIM=on, the IdP's pushed groups
# become teams and their members join and leave them. Off by default: /scim/v2/Groups lists none.
# SCIM_GROUPS=off

# Tiers (docs/adr/0033-tiers-model-routing.md): per-user plans that pick each Bot's model and set
# limits. Unset: one built-in tier, self-host, that allows any harness and model with no limits (as
# before). Copy deploy/tiers.example.json, edit it, and point TIERS_FILE at it. Restart to apply.
# TIERS_FILE=/home/superbot/superbot/tiers.json
# Tier for users without one of their own. Default: the file's "default", else its first tier.
# DEFAULT_TIER=starter
# Each tier's limits (docs/adr/0040-billing-v0-tier-limits.md): maxBots, monthlyRuns,
# dailyRoutineRuns (routine runs per UTC day), and machineType (the Linode plan its users' machines
# get). A limit left out is unlimited, and so is everything without TIERS_FILE.
# Billing (docs/adr/0068-no-free-tier.md): off while SuperBot is in testing. botd reads none of the
# STRIPE_* settings below, and makes no Stripe calls, unless BILLING=on. Never on with TESTING_MODE=on.
# BILLING=off
# Stripe (managed hosting): a signed webhook at /billing/stripe/webhook sets each user's tier from
# their subscription, by the tiers' "stripePrices". Off (404) unless BILLING=on and this is set; it can be a _FILE.
# Point the endpoint at customer.subscription.*, checkout.session.completed and customer.deleted.
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=<stripe-webhook-signing-secret>
# Checkout and the customer portal (docs/adr/0042-billing-checkout-portal-customers.md): with a
# Stripe secret or restricted key (sk_... or rk_...; it can be a _FILE), people buy plans with a price
# and manage their billing from the app's Plan screen. Off (404, no Plan screen) unless set, and needs
# STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET. A restricted key with write access to Checkout Sessions and Customer portal
# is enough. Never given to Bots.
# STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=<stripe-secret-key>
# Where Stripe sends people back (https, no path). Default: SSO_PUBLIC_URL, else the first ALLOWED_HOSTS.
# PUBLIC_URL=https://<your-host>
# Token metering (docs/adr/0050-billing-period-token-usage.md): botd always records each run's model
# tokens and enforces a tier's monthlyTokens. To also report them to Stripe for usage-based prices,
# set the event name of a Stripe meter (sum of values, customer mapped from stripe_customer_id).
# Off unless set; needs STRIPE_SECRET_KEY (a restricted key also needs write access to Meter Events).
# STRIPE_METER_EVENT=superbot_tokens
# Billing emails (docs/adr/0091-billing-emails.md): plan, payment and limit emails to each user,
# through approval emails' SMTP server (SMTP_HOST, SMTP_FROM, ...; docs/adr/0061-approval-notifications.md).
# On while BILLING=on and SMTP is set; off to stop them. Links go to PUBLIC_URL's Plan screen.
# BILLING_EMAILS=on
# Published plans (docs/adr/0121-published-plans.md): a public pricing page at /pricing and its JSON
# at /api/plans, built from TIERS_FILE: each tier with stripePrices or publishedPrices, its limits and
# the prices you write in its "publishedPrices" (botd never asks Stripe; no price shows "Contact us").
# Stripe price ids, models and routes are never shown. auto (default): on only while billing is on,
# so a self-hosted server with billing off publishes nothing; on: always; off: never (404).
# PLANS_PAGE=auto
# Where Enterprise buyers ask (an https:// page or mailto:). Unset: no Enterprise entry.
# PLANS_CONTACT_URL=https://<your-host>/contact

# License (docs/adr/0038-self-host-license-key.md, docs/adr/0068-no-free-tier.md). SuperBot has no
# free tier: a license from CG-Labs sets your plan and seats (active people, the owner account aside).
# Install it in the console (Settings), with `botctl license install < license.key`, or the
# installer's --license-file: all save DATA_DIR/license.key, no restart. Setting either line below
# instead wins over that file and needs a restart. A bad or ended key (after a 14-day grace period)
# never stops the server, but without a license Bots don't run and nobody new can be added.
# LICENSE_KEY=<license-key>
# LICENSE_KEY_FILE=/home/superbot/license.key
# Testing mode: run without a license, billing off, no seat limit. For test servers (like dogfood)
# only; the console, the setup wizard and the start-up log say so. Off unless set.
# TESTING_MODE=on
# Testing mode only, for tests that make a harness time its approval hook out: every hook times
# out after this many seconds (at least 2) and fails closed (docs/adr/0075-hermes-upgrades.md).
# Never on a real server: approvals would be blocked while people are still deciding.
# SUPERBOT_TEST_HOOK_TIMEOUT_SECS=3

# Managed hosting only: one Linode per user (docs/adr/0025-control-plane-linode.md). Off unless
# LINODE_TOKEN is set; self-hosted servers leave all of this commented out. The token can be a _FILE.
# LINODE_TOKEN=<linode-personal-access-token>
# MACHINE_REGIONS=gb-lon,de-fra-2
# MACHINE_TIERS=starter=g6-standard-1,pro=g6-standard-2,max=g6-standard-4
# MACHINE_IMAGE=linode/ubuntu24.04
# MACHINE_BACKUPS=on
# The most a Linode machine's newest Linode backup may lag, 25 to 168 (docs/adr/0110-linode-machine-recovery-objectives.md).
# MACHINE_BACKUPS_RPO_HOURS=26
# MACHINE_LABEL_PREFIX=superbot
# MACHINE_AUTHORIZED_KEYS=ssh-ed25519 AAAA... ops@example.com
# MACHINE_RECONCILE_SECONDS=60
# Tiers whose machines must have a kernel of their own (VMs), or all. botd won't start with a provider
# whose machines share their host's kernel, such as Incus containers, and refuses runs on older machines
# of those tiers that aren't its VMs (docs/adr/0120-managed-substrate.md).
# MACHINE_OWN_KERNEL_TIERS=all
# Or one unprivileged Incus container per user on a shared host (docs/adr/0065-incus-container-machines.md,
# docs/ops/incus-containers.md). Instead of LINODE_TOKEN; needs MACHINE_CONTROL_URL and
# MACHINE_FIREWALL=on. Plans are c<cpus>-m<memory GiB>-d<disk GiB>; default tiers
# starter=c1-m2-d20,pro=c2-m4-d40,max=c4-m8-d80, image ubuntu/24.04/cloud, region local.
# The client key can administer the Incus host: a server secret (_FILE works for all three PEMs).
# MACHINE_PROVIDER=incus
# INCUS_URL must be an IP address, so containers can be denied it.
# INCUS_URL=https://<incus-host-ip>:8443
# INCUS_CLIENT_CERT_FILE=/etc/superbot/incus/botd-incus.crt
# INCUS_CLIENT_KEY_FILE=/etc/superbot/incus/botd-incus.key
# INCUS_SERVER_CERT_FILE=/etc/superbot/incus/server.crt
# INCUS_PROJECT=superbot
# INCUS_NETWORK=incusbr0
# INCUS_STORAGE_POOL=default
# INCUS_IMAGE_SERVER=https://images.linuxcontainers.org
# Extra CIDRs containers may never reach, on top of private ranges and the Incus host.
# INCUS_EGRESS_DENY=<host-public-ip>/32
# Off-host backups of containers to S3-compatible storage (docs/adr/0071-off-host-machine-backups.md).
# Off unless the bucket is set; needs the endpoint and keys (secrets, _FILE works). HTTPS only.
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_BUCKET=<bucket>
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_ENDPOINT=https://<s3-endpoint>
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_REGION=us-east-1
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=<access-key-id>
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=<secret-access-key>
# MACHINE_BACKUP_S3_PREFIX=superbot/
# Encrypt to these age public keys (comma-separated); restores then need the identity.
# MACHINE_BACKUP_AGE_RECIPIENTS=age1...
# Hours between backups, recovery point and restore time objectives, per tier like retention
# (docs/adr/0074-machine-rpo-and-rto.md). Unset: every 24 h, pro 12, max 6; RPO = interval + 2 h;
# RTO 60 min. A set schedule replaces the default one; botd refuses an RPO under its interval + 1 h.
# MACHINE_BACKUP_INTERVAL_HOURS=24,pro=12,max=6
# MACHINE_BACKUP_RPO_HOURS=26,pro=14,max=8
# MACHINE_RESTORE_RTO_MINUTES=60
# Days kept: for every tier, or per tier (the bare number is the default). A machine's newest is always kept.
# MACHINE_BACKUP_RETENTION=7,pro=30
# Where Bots run (docs/adr/0036-bots-on-the-users-machine.md). off: on this server, in their owner's
# folders. assigned: users an admin assigned to their machine (botctl machine assign <user>) run
# there, everyone else here. on: every Bot runs on its owner's assigned machine, or is refused.
# An assigned user's Bots never fall back to this server. Needs machine enrollment below.
# MACHINE_ROUTING=off
# Shared Bots (docs/adr/0046-shared-bots-under-isolation.md). A shared Bot always runs on its
# owner's computer. isolated: a run for anyone but its owner reaches only Bots that person can,
# gets server-wide keys only (never the owner's own) and none of the owner's facts; new Bots are
# private. open: it acts as its owner for everyone, as before. Default: open with
# MACHINE_ROUTING=off, isolated otherwise (open can't be used with routing on).
# SHARED_BOTS=open
# Team accounts (docs/adr/0087-team-accounts-and-machines.md): a shared Bot, and a grant, reach
# only the owner's team. off (default): people in no team share with each other, as before.
# on: people in no team share with nobody.
# TEAMS=off
# How long a leaver's team machine stays suspended before it's destroyed (0 to 365; 0: at once).
# TEAM_MACHINE_DAYS=7
# A disabled or deprovisioned user's own machine (docs/adr/0124-deprovisioning-stops-bots-and-machines.md).
# Their Bots' runs stop either way. suspend (default): powered off at once, and back on when
# they're enabled again (never one an admin suspended by hand). keep: left running, unreachable.
# MACHINE_ON_DISABLE=suspend
# A disabled user's routines (docs/adr/0047-disabled-users-routines.md) are paused when they're
# disabled. paused: re-enabling them leaves those paused until an admin resumes them
# (botctl user resume-routines <user>). resume: re-enabling turns them back on.
# ROUTINES_ON_ENABLE=paused
# One Unix account per user on this server (docs/adr/0037-per-user-unix-accounts.md,
# docs/adr/0058-harness-os-user-by-default.md): each user's Bots run as sb-u-<user id>, in
# ISOLATION_DIR/<user id>, with their own harness logins, and can't read this file, DATA_DIR or the
# master key. Needs root's setup: deploy/install.sh does it for new installs (--isolation for an
# existing one) and sets ISOLATION=users here. Off: every Bot runs as this account.
# ISOLATION=off
# ISOLATION_DIR=/srv/superbot/users
# A teammate's account for an owner's shared Bots goes when they leave or lose those Bots
# (docs/adr/0066-removing-teammate-accounts-on-one-server.md). Their work: archive (default) hands
# it to the owner, in workspace/from-teammates/; delete holds it aside, then deletes it after
# ISOLATION_TEAMMATE_FILES_DAYS (0 to 365, default 7; 0 deletes at once).
# ISOLATION_TEAMMATE_FILES=archive
# ISOLATION_TEAMMATE_FILES_DAYS=7
# Machine enrollment and mutual TLS (docs/adr/0031-machine-enrollment-mtls.md). Off unless
# MACHINE_CONTROL_URL is set (needs LINODE_TOKEN or MACHINE_PROVIDER=incus). The URL is how machines reach this server; botd
# listens there on its own port (open it in the firewall). The CA comes from the master key.
# MACHINE_CONTROL_URL=https://<server-ip>:8443
# MACHINE_LISTEN=0.0.0.0:8443
# MACHINE_AGENT_PORT=7443
# Minutes a new machine has to enroll before it is marked overdue and audited (5 to 60).
# MACHINE_ENROLL_TIMEOUT_MINUTES=15
# Harness CLIs every machine's agent installs by itself (docs/adr/0039-machine-harnesses-and-agent-upgrades.md):
# any of claude, codex, pi, hermes, at pinned versions checked by hash. name@version picks another
# exact version (name@version=<checksum> pins its hash). Unset: none are installed.
# MACHINE_HARNESSES=claude,codex,pi
# manual (default): an agent is upgraded to botd's build only when an admin asks
# (botctl machine upgrade <user>). auto: every idle agent is, once per build. Admins can hold one.
# MACHINE_AGENT_UPGRADES=manual
# Machines enrolled before agent upgrades can't update themselves; botd rebuilds them in place with
# today's agent (docs/adr/0056-rebuilding-machines-enrolled-before-0039.md). It wipes their disks.
# manual (default): only when an admin asks (botctl machine rebuild <user>). auto: also every idle one
# that was never assigned and never ran a Bot. off: never.
# MACHINE_LEGACY_REBUILDS=manual
# Harness logins and the user's folder on their machine (docs/adr/0041-machine-harness-logins-and-folder-copy.md).
# Both off by default; both need MACHINE_CONTROL_URL. on: Claude Code and Codex log in on the machine
# (botctl machine harness-login <user> claude), and runs of them without a login there or an API key
# are refused. The credentials stay on the machine.
# MACHINE_HARNESS_LOGINS=on
# on: assigning a user copies their folder to their machine (checked, resumable, never over their
# changes there). A folder over MACHINE_FOLDER_SYNC_MAX_MB (default 2048) isn't copied.
# MACHINE_FOLDER_SYNC=on
# MACHINE_FOLDER_SYNC_MAX_MB=2048
# Bots' own OS user on their machine (docs/adr/0043-bots-own-os-user-on-machines.md). Off by default;
# needs MACHINE_CONTROL_URL. on: each idle agent is asked to run its Bots, harness CLIs, logins and
# folder copy as the unprivileged superbot-bots user (the agent stays root). Runs wait until it's
# done; older agents must upgrade first. Turning it off again doesn't switch a machine back.
# MACHINE_BOT_USER=on
# Bots' screens on their machine (docs/adr/0045-screens-on-machines.md). Off by default; needs
# MACHINE_BOT_USER=on. on: a Bot routed to its owner's machine gets its screen there (Xvfb, Openbox,
# D-Bus and voxl as superbot-bots), shown at /screen/<port>/ through the agent's mutual-TLS port. No
# new port opens on the machine. Off, such a Bot has no screen; it never falls back to this server's.
# botd listens on 127.0.0.1 at MACHINE_SCREEN_PORTS (within the proxy's 6900-6999 /screen/ route,
# clear of SCREEN_BASE's ports). SCREEN_SIZE and SCREEN_IDLE_MINUTES apply there too.
# MACHINE_SCREENS=on
# MACHINE_SCREEN_PORTS=6960-6999
# Teammates' runs of shared Bots as their own OS user (docs/adr/0048-teammates-own-os-user-on-machines.md).
# Off by default; needs MACHINE_BOT_USER=on. on: a run of a shared Bot for someone other than its owner
# runs on the owner's machine as that teammate's own user (superbot-t-...), in their own home, and can't
# read the owner's folder or logins. Claude Code and Codex then need a server-wide key for it. Each
# person gets their own screen of the Bot; every display there takes an X cookie. Agents must be current.
# MACHINE_TEAMMATE_USERS=on
# A teammate who leaves (disabled, deprovisioned, or no longer able to run the owner's Bots) loses their
# user, home and processes on the owner's machine (docs/adr/0052-teammate-offboarding-on-machines.md).
# Their work folder: archive (default) moves it to the owner's work/from-teammates/; delete holds it
# aside, then deletes it after MACHINE_TEAMMATE_FILES_DAYS (0 to 365, default 7; 0 deletes at once).
# MACHINE_TEAMMATE_FILES=archive
# MACHINE_TEAMMATE_FILES_DAYS=7
# A Linode Cloud Firewall in front of every machine (docs/adr/0035-machine-firewall-auto-enroll.md).
# Off by default; needs MACHINE_CONTROL_URL and a LINODE_TOKEN with Firewalls read/write. Inbound is
# dropped but for MACHINE_AGENT_PORT from this server (the URL's address, or the sources below).
# SSH stays closed unless MACHINE_SSH_SOURCES is set. Addresses are CIDRs, comma-separated.
# MACHINE_FIREWALL=on
# MACHINE_FIREWALL_SOURCES=<server-ip>/32
# MACHINE_SSH_SOURCES=<your-ip>/32
# Machines hardened at first boot (docs/adr/0126-per-user-machine-hardening.md). On by default with
# MACHINE_CONTROL_URL; applies to new and rebuilt machines. SSH takes only root with a key, no
# forwarding; kernel hardening sysctls; unattended security updates, never rebooting by themselves;
# and egress rules on the machine: every user but root and the OS's network daemons (so every Bot and
# teammate user, even one made later) can't reach private, shared, link-local or metadata addresses,
# nor MACHINE_EGRESS_DENY (CIDRs, comma-separated). Bots still reach botd through the agent. Those
# rules bind Bots only with MACHINE_BOT_USER=on: a Bot that runs as root could lift them.
# MACHINE_HARDENING=on
# MACHINE_EGRESS_DENY=<office-range>/24
# Each Bot run in a cgroup of its own, held to its tier's runLimits (docs/adr/0135-run-limits-and-egress-allow-lists.md):
# a share of the machine's CPUs and memory, a process cap and an IO weight. On by default with
# MACHINE_CONTROL_URL; applies to new and rebuilt machines. Outbound allow-lists are set by admins at
# runtime (botctl machine egress, botctl user egress), not here.
# MACHINE_RUN_LIMITS=on

# Name of the primary Bot created on first start.
PRIMARY_BOT_NAME=Assistant

# Bot screens (docs/computer.md). On when Xvfb and openbox are installed.
SCREENS=on
MAX_SCREENS=3
SCREEN_IDLE_MINUTES=10
# WIDTHxHEIGHT (320x240 to 7680x4320). Anything else falls back to 1280x800.
SCREEN_SIZE=1280x800
# Displays are :SCREEN_BASE+n, voxl ports 6800+SCREEN_BASE+n. Change it to run two servers on one machine.
# The ports must stay within 6900-6999 (the proxy's /screen/ route), or botd won't start.
SCREEN_BASE=100
AGENT_BROWSER_SKILLS_DIR=/opt/computer/share/agent-browser-skills
